SETTING THE POSITION.X OF A TRANSFORM
You may wonder why you can't just set go.transforP.position.[ aYg;
in the $lign;() function of Code Listing 24.7. Instead, you must first copy the
transforP.position to a Vector3 variable (e.g., 9ector tePpPos), then change
the tePpPos.[ value, and finally copy the whole 9ector back onto transforP.
position.
The reason for this is that transforP.position is a property, not an actual field
(a field is a class variable). Properties are functions that are masquerading as fields.
So, when you set the value of transforP.position, rather than setting the
value of a field, you're actually passing a Vector3 to a function that is doing some
behind-the-scenes work to move that Transform to world coordinates that match
the x, y, and z values of that Vector3. You can't directly set the value transforP.
position.[ because of the way that properties work in C#.
